<figure class="incident-figure" aria-labelledby="request-time-title">
  <figcaption id="request-time-title"><span class="figure-number">Figure 1</span> One request, several intervals</figcaption>
  <div class="incident-scene time-scene">
    <p class="scene-note">Illustrative elapsed time · 0 to 1,000 ms</p>
    <div class="time-row"><span>Request</span><div class="track"><span class="interval whole" style="left:0%;width:100%">1,000 ms</span></div></div>
    <div class="time-row"><span>Get connection</span><div class="track"><span class="interval" style="left:0%;width:60%">600 ms</span></div></div>
    <div class="time-row"><span>Statement</span><div class="track"><span class="interval" style="left:60%;width:30%">300 ms</span></div></div>
    <div class="time-row"><span>Return result</span><div class="track"><span class="interval" style="left:90%;width:10%"></span></div></div>
    <p class="scene-note last-part">Return result: final 100 ms. Statement time can itself include waits.</p>
    <p class="figure-explanation">600 + 300 + 100 = 1,000 ms. The statement’s 300 ms is already inside the request’s 1,000 ms.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="incident-caption">A simplified request with consecutive stages. The first interval begins when the application asks its pool for a connection; the last includes receiving, processing and sending the result. Real tracing boundaries and overlapping work depend on the application.</p>
</figure>
